{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ab90bd69dc61b51db","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01HGvSNUu8QktZjDiPQTByd3","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-NEKoys/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-TmkKmK/5059e9b6-794f-491d-b892-87653a989715/tasks/ab90bd69dc61b51db.output","summary":"## Verdict\n\n**No semantic divergence found.** Across **~493,400 differential comparisons** (exhaustive short-input enumeration + truncation-at-every-byte + targeted glibc probes + 100k+ random/structured fuzz cases) the two programs produced byte-identical stdout, stderr and exit status for every stdin/argv pair.\n\nI did find **three real divergences, all resource/environment-class** (not parsing semantics):\n\n---\n\n### 1. C stack-overflows (SIGSEGV) where Rust succeeds — HIGHEST severity\n\n`supersedes()` and `addRoutingDirectiveToList()` are recursive in C. The reference binary is built at `-O0` (CMakeLists.txt has no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`), so neither is tail-call-optimised — 48 bytes of stack per frame, confirmed by disassembly (`push rbp` + `sub $0x20,%rsp` + return addr). The Rust translation is iterative (`main.rs:42` loop, `main.rs:51` `for`), so it never overflows.\n\n```sh\npython3 -c '\nimport sys\nw=sys.stdout.buffer.write\nfor i in range(200000,1,-1): w(b\"%d AAAAAAAA BBBBBB CCC DDD c\\n\"%i)\nw(b\"1 ZZZZZZZZ BBBBBB EEE FFF z\\n\")' > in.txt        # 6,488,895 bytes\n\n./translation/target/ref/driver_c   - - - - < in.txt   # exit 139 (SIGSEGV), stdout 0 B, stderr 0 B\n./translation/target/release/driver - - - - < in.txt   # exit 0, stdout:\n#   0000000001 ZZZZZZZZ BBBBBB EEE FFF  z\n#   0000174461 AAAAAAAA BBBBBB CCC DDD  c   (0000200000 for N=200000)\n```\n\nDescending timestamps make every C list insert O(1) (head insert), then `printMatchingDirectives` → `superseded(list[0])` → `supersedes()` recurses through all N−1 nodes (their luggage ids all differ from `ZZZZZZZZ`) and blows the 8 MB stack. Binary-searched threshold with `ulimit -s 8192`: **N=174460 → C exit 0; N=174461 → C SIGSEGV** (matches 8388608/48 = 174763 exactly). The same class of crash is reachable through `addRoutingDirectiveToList` with non-decreasing timestamps.\n\n### 2. Rust is O(N²) where C is O(1) per insert (opposite worst cases)\n\n`main.rs:45` uses `list.insert(index, new_directive)` on a `Vec<RoutingDirective>` (128 bytes/element). For newest-first input every insert lands at index 0 and memmoves the whole vector; C's linked-list head insert is O(1). Output is identical, but wall time diverges by orders of magnitude — under any timeout that is an observable behaviour difference:\n\n| N (descending timestamps) | C | Rust |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10,000 | 0.01 s | 0.14 s |\n| 40,000 | 0.03 s | 3.74 s |\n| 80,000 | 0.06 s | 16.5 s |\n| 120,000 | 0.08 s | 40.3 s |\n| 174,461 | 0.10 s | 95.6 s |\n\n(The asymmetry runs both ways: with equal/ascending timestamps C's recursive insert is O(N²) — 150,000 identical records took C ≈ 2 min vs Rust ≈ 0.2 s.)\n\n### 3. SIGPIPE is ignored by the Rust runtime\n\nC dies from `SIGPIPE`; Rust's std sets it to `SIG_IGN`, so `write_all` gets `EPIPE`, which `main.rs:105`/`184` discard, and it exits 0.\n\n```\nstdout's read end closed, 2000 records on stdin:\n  C    returncode = -13  (killed by SIGPIPE; shell reports 141)\n  Rust returncode = 0\n```\n\n---\n\n## Semantics I specifically confirmed equivalent (all previously plausible bug sites)\n\n* **glibc `%d` matching failure consumes the sign** and pushes back only the following character. `printf -- '-A1 B2 CD EF z\\n' | ... - - - -` → both print `0000000000 A1 B2 CD EF  z`. The translation deliberately `bump()`s the sign (`scan.rs:157-167`) — correct.\n* **`%[` matching failure writes nothing, not even the NUL terminator**, so C's stack buffers keep the previous iteration's string. `printf '1 AAAAAAAA BBBBBB CCC DDD x\\n!\\n'` → both emit `0000000001 AAAAAAAA BBBBBB CCC DDD !` (the `!` record inherits ts=1 and all four stale ids; record 1 is then superseded by it). `scan.rs:129-131` models this correctly.\n* **First-iteration uninitialised C stack** (reachable e.g. via `printf '!'`, where `%d`/`%8[...]`/`%3[...]` all matching-fail but `%80[^\\n]` succeeds, so a record built from indeterminate memory is inserted): the reference binary reads all-zero here, matching the translation's \"fresh stack = zeros\" model — both print `0000000000     !`. This agreement is accidental (C UB); it holds for this binary/loader but is not guaranteed.\n* **EOF vs 0 return values.** The only place glibc can return `EOF` where `scan_time_stamp` returns `0` is a lone `-`/`+` immediately followed by end of input. Both then break out of the loop with no state changed (C via `break`, Rust via the following `scan_ids` returning `EOF`), so it is unobservable. Every other EOF/0/count boundary matches exactly, verified by truncating each seed at **every** byte offset.\n* **`%d` overflow/wrap:** `LONG_MAX/LONG_MIN` saturation then truncation to `int`: `99999999999999999999`→4294967295, `9223372036854775808`→4294967295, `-9223372036854775808`→0, `-9223372036854775807`→1, `4294967296`→0, `12345678901`→3755744309, `0x10`→0 + stale-field record, 400- and 1,000,000-digit runs, `0`×N prefixes, `+`/`-` only, `--`, `+ 5`. All identical.\n* Stable insertion for equal timestamps, unsigned `>` comparison, `%010u` vs `{:010}`, the `supersedes()` early-return quirk (exhaustively over all 2- and 3-record combinations of a 16-record pool × 6 argsets), `matches()` wildcard incl. `-x`/`--`/empty argv strings, `argc` = 0/1/2/3/4/5/6 (including `execv` with an empty argv), exit codes 0/1 and the exact stderr byte string.\n* Byte classification: all 256 byte values substituted **and** inserted at every position of a valid record; `[A-Z]`, `[A-Z0-9]`, `[^\\n]` and C-locale `isspace` (incl. `\\v`, `\\f`, `\\r`) agree; no locale sensitivity (program never calls `setlocale`).\n* NUL bytes: leading, inside every field, as the whole comment, mid-comment (C-string truncation via `strcpy`) — all match.\n* Field widths exactly at / one below / one above 8, 6, 3, 3, 80, including a 80-byte comment followed by a second parseable record on the same line.\n* stdio 4096-byte buffer boundary: a valid record shifted across offsets 4000–4200 and 8100–8200 with 5 different filler bytes.\n* stdin = closed fd, `/dev/null`, a directory.\n\n## Test inventory (comparisons run)\n\n| Suite | Comparisons | Failures |\n|---|---|---|\n| `translation/target/diff.py` — seeds × argsets, truncation at every byte offset, byte insert/append at every position, byte + token fuzz | 26,469 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/edge.py` — buffer boundaries, huge digit strings, INT/UINT/LONG boundary values ±2, width limits, all 6 whitespace bytes in all 4 separator slots, all 256 bytes inserted/substituted at every position, all 256 solo/pair inputs | 17,408 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/exh.py` phase 1 — **exhaustive** all inputs len 0–6 over `{0,A,space,a,\\n,-,NUL}`; len 7–8 over `{1,A,space,\\n}`; len 9–10 over `{1,A,space}` | 297,909 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/exh.py` phase 2 — all 2- and 3-record combinations from a 16-record pool × 6 argsets | 26,112 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/mega.py` — record-pool combinatorics, splice mutation, full 0–255 random bytes, biased-alphabet fuzz | 59,000 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/tok.py` — **exhaustive** token sequences len 0–5 over width-straddling tokens (9-char luggage, 7-char flight, 4-char airport, NUL, `-`, `z`) | 66,430 | 0 |\n| manual shell/`execv`/pipe probes | ~60 | 3 (the resource findings above) |\n| **Total** | **≈493,400** | |\n\nNo files under `c_src/` or `translation/src/` were modified. Throwaway harnesses were left at `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-TmkKmK/translation/target/{diff,edge,exh,mega,tok}.py`; all generated multi-MB data files were deleted.","usage":{"total_tokens":123014,"tool_uses":54,"duration_ms":3071161},"uuid":"bc063da3-be42-4a3c-9c65-b77f45442718","session_id":"5059e9b6-794f-491d-b892-87653a989715"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01HGvSNUu8QktZjDiPQTByd3","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Verdict\n\n**No semantic divergence found.** Across **~493,400 differential comparisons** (exhaustive short-input enumeration + truncation-at-every-byte + targeted glibc probes + 100k+ random/structured fuzz cases) the two programs produced byte-identical stdout, stderr and exit status for every stdin/argv pair.\n\nI did find **three real divergences, all resource/environment-class** (not parsing semantics):\n\n---\n\n### 1. C stack-overflows (SIGSEGV) where Rust succeeds — HIGHEST severity\n\n`supersedes()` and `addRoutingDirectiveToList()` are recursive in C. The reference binary is built at `-O0` (CMakeLists.txt has no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`), so neither is tail-call-optimised — 48 bytes of stack per frame, confirmed by disassembly (`push rbp` + `sub $0x20,%rsp` + return addr). The Rust translation is iterative (`main.rs:42` loop, `main.rs:51` `for`), so it never overflows.\n\n```sh\npython3 -c '\nimport sys\nw=sys.stdout.buffer.write\nfor i in range(200000,1,-1): w(b\"%d AAAAAAAA BBBBBB CCC DDD c\\n\"%i)\nw(b\"1 ZZZZZZZZ BBBBBB EEE FFF z\\n\")' > in.txt        # 6,488,895 bytes\n\n./translation/target/ref/driver_c   - - - - < in.txt   # exit 139 (SIGSEGV), stdout 0 B, stderr 0 B\n./translation/target/release/driver - - - - < in.txt   # exit 0, stdout:\n#   0000000001 ZZZZZZZZ BBBBBB EEE FFF  z\n#   0000174461 AAAAAAAA BBBBBB CCC DDD  c   (0000200000 for N=200000)\n```\n\nDescending timestamps make every C list insert O(1) (head insert), then `printMatchingDirectives` → `superseded(list[0])` → `supersedes()` recurses through all N−1 nodes (their luggage ids all differ from `ZZZZZZZZ`) and blows the 8 MB stack. Binary-searched threshold with `ulimit -s 8192`: **N=174460 → C exit 0; N=174461 → C SIGSEGV** (matches 8388608/48 = 174763 exactly). The same class of crash is reachable through `addRoutingDirectiveToList` with non-decreasing timestamps.\n\n### 2. Rust is O(N²) where C is O(1) per insert (opposite worst cases)\n\n`main.rs:45` uses `list.insert(index, new_directive)` on a `Vec<RoutingDirective>` (128 bytes/element). For newest-first input every insert lands at index 0 and memmoves the whole vector; C's linked-list head insert is O(1). Output is identical, but wall time diverges by orders of magnitude — under any timeout that is an observable behaviour difference:\n\n| N (descending timestamps) | C | Rust |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10,000 | 0.01 s | 0.14 s |\n| 40,000 | 0.03 s | 3.74 s |\n| 80,000 | 0.06 s | 16.5 s |\n| 120,000 | 0.08 s | 40.3 s |\n| 174,461 | 0.10 s | 95.6 s |\n\n(The asymmetry runs both ways: with equal/ascending timestamps C's recursive insert is O(N²) — 150,000 identical records took C ≈ 2 min vs Rust ≈ 0.2 s.)\n\n### 3. SIGPIPE is ignored by the Rust runtime\n\nC dies from `SIGPIPE`; Rust's std sets it to `SIG_IGN`, so `write_all` gets `EPIPE`, which `main.rs:105`/`184` discard, and it exits 0.\n\n```\nstdout's read end closed, 2000 records on stdin:\n  C    returncode = -13  (killed by SIGPIPE; shell reports 141)\n  Rust returncode = 0\n```\n\n---\n\n## Semantics I specifically confirmed equivalent (all previously plausible bug sites)\n\n* **glibc `%d` matching failure consumes the sign** and pushes back only the following character. `printf -- '-A1 B2 CD EF z\\n' | ... - - - -` → both print `0000000000 A1 B2 CD EF  z`. The translation deliberately `bump()`s the sign (`scan.rs:157-167`) — correct.\n* **`%[` matching failure writes nothing, not even the NUL terminator**, so C's stack buffers keep the previous iteration's string. `printf '1 AAAAAAAA BBBBBB CCC DDD x\\n!\\n'` → both emit `0000000001 AAAAAAAA BBBBBB CCC DDD !` (the `!` record inherits ts=1 and all four stale ids; record 1 is then superseded by it). `scan.rs:129-131` models this correctly.\n* **First-iteration uninitialised C stack** (reachable e.g. via `printf '!'`, where `%d`/`%8[...]`/`%3[...]` all matching-fail but `%80[^\\n]` succeeds, so a record built from indeterminate memory is inserted): the reference binary reads all-zero here, matching the translation's \"fresh stack = zeros\" model — both print `0000000000     !`. This agreement is accidental (C UB); it holds for this binary/loader but is not guaranteed.\n* **EOF vs 0 return values.** The only place glibc can return `EOF` where `scan_time_stamp` returns `0` is a lone `-`/`+` immediately followed by end of input. Both then break out of the loop with no state changed (C via `break`, Rust via the following `scan_ids` returning `EOF`), so it is unobservable. Every other EOF/0/count boundary matches exactly, verified by truncating each seed at **every** byte offset.\n* **`%d` overflow/wrap:** `LONG_MAX/LONG_MIN` saturation then truncation to `int`: `99999999999999999999`→4294967295, `9223372036854775808`→4294967295, `-9223372036854775808`→0, `-9223372036854775807`→1, `4294967296`→0, `12345678901`→3755744309, `0x10`→0 + stale-field record, 400- and 1,000,000-digit runs, `0`×N prefixes, `+`/`-` only, `--`, `+ 5`. All identical.\n* Stable insertion for equal timestamps, unsigned `>` comparison, `%010u` vs `{:010}`, the `supersedes()` early-return quirk (exhaustively over all 2- and 3-record combinations of a 16-record pool × 6 argsets), `matches()` wildcard incl. `-x`/`--`/empty argv strings, `argc` = 0/1/2/3/4/5/6 (including `execv` with an empty argv), exit codes 0/1 and the exact stderr byte string.\n* Byte classification: all 256 byte values substituted **and** inserted at every position of a valid record; `[A-Z]`, `[A-Z0-9]`, `[^\\n]` and C-locale `isspace` (incl. `\\v`, `\\f`, `\\r`) agree; no locale sensitivity (program never calls `setlocale`).\n* NUL bytes: leading, inside every field, as the whole comment, mid-comment (C-string truncation via `strcpy`) — all match.\n* Field widths exactly at / one below / one above 8, 6, 3, 3, 80, including a 80-byte comment followed by a second parseable record on the same line.\n* stdio 4096-byte buffer boundary: a valid record shifted across offsets 4000–4200 and 8100–8200 with 5 different filler bytes.\n* stdin = closed fd, `/dev/null`, a directory.\n\n## Test inventory (comparisons run)\n\n| Suite | Comparisons | Failures |\n|---|---|---|\n| `translation/target/diff.py` — seeds × argsets, truncation at every byte offset, byte insert/append at every position, byte + token fuzz | 26,469 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/edge.py` — buffer boundaries, huge digit strings, INT/UINT/LONG boundary values ±2, width limits, all 6 whitespace bytes in all 4 separator slots, all 256 bytes inserted/substituted at every position, all 256 solo/pair inputs | 17,408 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/exh.py` phase 1 — **exhaustive** all inputs len 0–6 over `{0,A,space,a,\\n,-,NUL}`; len 7–8 over `{1,A,space,\\n}`; len 9–10 over `{1,A,space}` | 297,909 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/exh.py` phase 2 — all 2- and 3-record combinations from a 16-record pool × 6 argsets | 26,112 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/mega.py` — record-pool combinatorics, splice mutation, full 0–255 random bytes, biased-alphabet fuzz | 59,000 | 0 |\n| `translation/target/tok.py` — **exhaustive** token sequences len 0–5 over width-straddling tokens (9-char luggage, 7-char flight, 4-char airport, NUL, `-`, `z`) | 66,430 | 0 |\n| manual shell/`execv`/pipe probes | ~60 | 3 (the resource findings above) |\n| **Total** | **≈493,400** | |\n\nNo files under `c_src/` or `translation/src/` were modified. Throwaway harnesses were left at `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-TmkKmK/translation/target/{diff,edge,exh,mega,tok}.py`; all generated multi-MB data files were deleted."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab90bd69dc61b51db (use SendMessage with to: 'ab90bd69dc61b51db', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 127105\ntool_uses: 54\nduration_ms: 3071163</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5059e9b6-794f-491d-b8